You are debugging a production issue where a high-throughput microservice intermittently runs out of memory (`java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space`). A custom in-memory cache was implemented using `java.util.WeakHashMap` to hold metadata about active `UserSession` objects:

java
@Component
public class SessionCache {
    private final Map<UserSession, SessionMetadata> cache = Collections.synchronizedMap(new WeakHashMap<>());

    public void cacheSession(UserSession session, String role) {
        cache.put(session, new SessionMetadata(session, role));
    }

    static class SessionMetadata {
        private final UserSession sessionRef;
        private final String role;

        public SessionMetadata(UserSession sessionRef, String role) {
            this.sessionRef = sessionRef;
            this.role = role;
        }
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where a concurrent batch processor stalls indefinitely, pegging CPU utilization at 100% on specific worker threads. The developer attempted to implement a custom cache utilizing `ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent()` to fetch or compute child collections dynamically:

java
@Service
public class HierarchyService {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, List<String>> hierarchyCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public List<String> getHierarchy(String nodeKey) {
        return hierarchyCache.computeIfAbsent(nodeKey, k -> {
            if (k.startsWith("CHILD_")) {
                return List.of(k);
            }
            // Recursively resolve child dependencies
            List<String> childNodes = getHierarchy("CHILD_" + k);
            return new ArrayList<>(childNodes);
        });
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where updates to a shared collection managed by a custom transactional component appear to be silently dropped or rolled back, despite successful execution of the business method:

java
@Service
public class AuditLogManager {
    private final Set<String> persistentBatch = new ConcurrentSkipListSet<>();

    public void queueAudit(String entry) {
        this.processAndPersist(entry);
    }

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void processAndPersist(String entry) {
        persistentBatch.add(entry);
        // Flush to database...
    }
}

Calls to `queueAudit()` execute without throwing exceptions, but database rollback logs reveal that transaction boundaries were completely ignored when exceptions occurred in downstream callers.
